    "description": "Knowledge graph of Frantz Fanon's The Wretched of the Earth (1961). Captures the conceptual structure (colonial Manichaeism, decolonizing violence, national consciousness and its pitfalls, négritude critique, mental disorders as colonial symptom), the figures Fanon engages with (Sartre, Bhabha, Marx, Césaire, the racist colonial-medicine school of Porot/Carothers), and four of the patient case studies from chapter V as reified n-ary events. Extracted from the 2004 Richard Philcox translation by Claude (i2t project) following the InstaGraph schema with the project's two extensions: edges may use `members` for n-ary hyperedges, and edges may carry an explicit `id` matching a node id for reification. Source text: ~/Knowledge/The Wretched Of The Earth.pdf (404 pages, extracted via pdftotext).",
    "source": "Frantz Fanon, The Wretched of the Earth (1961, Philcox tr. 2004); foreword by Homi K. Bhabha; preface by Jean-Paul Sartre",
    "extractor": "claude (claude-opus-4-7), schema-compatible with yoheinakajima/instagraph + i2t hyperedge/reification extensions"
  },
  "nodes": [
    {"id": "fanon", "label": "Frantz Fanon", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"lifespan": "1925-1961", "born": "Martinique", "role": "author of the book; psychiatrist; FLN militant; the philosophical-political voice"}},
    {"id": "sartre", "label": "Jean-Paul Sartre", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "wrote the 1961 preface; existentialist interlocutor; endorses Fanon's call for revolutionary violence"}},
    {"id": "bhabha", "label": "Homi K. Bhabha", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "wrote the 2004 foreword 'Framing Fanon'; postcolonial theorist re-introducing Fanon"}},
    {"id": "cesaire", "label": "Aimé Césaire", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "Martinican poet; co-founder of negritude; Fanon's teacher"}},
    {"id": "senghor", "label": "Léopold Sédar Senghor", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "Senegalese poet/politician; co-founder of negritude"}},
    {"id": "marx", "label": "Karl Marx", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "Fanon stretches Marx for the colonial context — race functions as class infrastructure"}},
    {"id": "lenin", "label": "V.I. Lenin", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "theorist of revolution Fanon engages with"}},
    {"id": "freud", "label": "Sigmund Freud", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "Fanon invokes 'Mourning and Melancholia' for case 3 analysis"}},
    {"id": "de_beauvoir", "label": "Simone de Beauvoir", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "friend; witness to Fanon's last days; quoted in foreword"}},
    {"id": "biko", "label": "Steve Biko", "type": "Theorist", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "South African anti-apartheid; Bhabha invokes him as one heir to Fanon"}},
    {"id": "de_gaulle", "label": "Charles de Gaulle", "type": "ColonialOfficial", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "French president; quoted speaking of 'yellow multitudes' as colonial vocabulary"}},
    {"id": "mauriac", "label": "François Mauriac", "type": "ColonialOfficial", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "French writer; quoted speaking of 'black, brown, and yellow hordes'"}},
    {"id": "meyer", "label": "Monsieur Meyer", "type": "ColonialOfficial", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "French Assembly speaker who said the Republic should not be 'defiled' by the Algerian people"}},
    {"id": "porot", "label": "Antoine Porot", "type": "ColonialMedic", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "Algiers School of Psychiatry founder; built the 'doctrine of primitivism' Fanon dismantles"}},
    {"id": "sutter", "label": "Sutter", "type": "ColonialMedic", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "Porot's student; co-author of the 1939 paper claiming Algerians are diencephalon-governed"}},
    {"id": "carothers", "label": "Dr. Carothers", "type": "ColonialMedic", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "WHO 'international expert' who claimed the normal African is a lobotomized European; 'analyzed' the Mau-Mau revolt as frustration complex"}},
    {"id": "baruk", "label": "Baruk", "type": "ColonialMedic", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "psychiatrist whose hysteria report Porot used as platform"}},
    {"id": "josie_fanon", "label": "Josie Fanon", "type": "Person", "color": "#F8C8DC", "properties": {"role": "Fanon's wife; read him reviews of Wretched on his deathbed"}},

    {"id": "wretched_book", "label": "The Wretched of the Earth", "type": "Work", "color": "#C8E0F8", "properties": {"year": 1961, "role": "the book itself; reified — Sartre prefaces it, Bhabha forewords it, the French police seized it on the day Fanon died"}},
    {"id": "black_skin", "label": "Black Skin, White Masks", "type": "Work", "color": "#C8E0F8", "properties": {"year": 1952, "role": "Fanon's earlier work on the psychic violence of colonial racism in metropolitan France"}},
    {"id": "sartre_preface", "label": "Sartre's Preface (1961)", "type": "Work", "color": "#C8E0F8", "properties": {"role": "the preface that radicalized European reception; reified — Bhabha discusses it"}},
    {"id": "bhabha_foreword", "label": "Bhabha's Foreword 'Framing Fanon' (2004)", "type": "Work", "color": "#C8E0F8", "properties": {"role": "frame around the Philcox retranslation; reads Fanon for the post-Bandung-collapse era"}},
    {"id": "mourning_and_melancholia", "label": "Mourning and Melancholia", "type": "Work", "color": "#C8E0F8", "properties": {"role": "Freud's essay on guilt-after-loss; Fanon invokes it analyzing case 3"}},
    {"id": "force_of_circumstance", "label": "The Force of Circumstance", "type": "Work", "color": "#C8E0F8", "properties": {"role": "Beauvoir's memoir; quoted by Bhabha for Fanon's 'tuned antennae' remark"}},
    {"id": "porot_doctrine", "label": "Porot's doctrine of primitivism", "type": "ReifiedClaim", "color": "#C8E0F8", "properties": {"role": "the racist scientific edifice claiming North Africans are biologically diencephalon-governed; reified — Fanon attacks it as a node"}},
    {"id": "carothers_doctrine", "label": "Carothers's 'lobotomized European' claim", "type": "ReifiedClaim", "color": "#C8E0F8", "properties": {"role": "WHO-endorsed claim that the normal African resembles a lobotomized European; reified"}},

    {"id": "decolonization", "label": "Decolonization", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the central process the book theorizes; described as necessarily violent because colonialism was"}},
    {"id": "violence", "label": "Violence (revolutionary)", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "Fanon's controversial thesis: anticolonial violence is not optional moral excess but the structurally necessary means by which the colonized re-becomes a person"}},
    {"id": "colonial_manichaeism", "label": "Colonial Manichaeism", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the colonial world is divided into two mutually exclusive sectors with no possible mediation"}},
    {"id": "compartmentalized_world", "label": "the Compartmentalized World", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the spatial form Manichaeism takes — colonist sector vs native sector, with police and army at the boundary"}},
    {"id": "colonist_sector", "label": "the Colonist's Sector", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "stone, steel, paved roads, full bellies, white folks; built to last"}},
    {"id": "native_sector", "label": "the Native's Sector", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "shanty town, Medina, reservation; famished, prostrate, on its knees"}},
    {"id": "national_consciousness", "label": "National Consciousness", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the political-cultural awakening that organizes the struggle; chapter III subjects it to critique"}},
    {"id": "national_bourgeoisie", "label": "the National Bourgeoisie", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the comprador class that inherits colonial structures and reproduces them; the 'pitfall' of national consciousness"}},
    {"id": "peasantry", "label": "the Peasantry", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "Fanon's revolutionary class — the peasants outside the colonial wage system"}},
    {"id": "lumpenproletariat", "label": "the Lumpenproletariat", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the displaced rural-to-urban poor; Fanon (against orthodoxy) sees them as revolutionary potential"}},
    {"id": "spontaneity", "label": "Spontaneity", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the revolt's initial energy; chapter II names both its strength and its weakness when not organized"}},
    {"id": "national_culture", "label": "National Culture", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "chapter IV: culture rebuilt through and after the liberation struggle, not retrieved as static heritage"}},
    {"id": "negritude", "label": "Négritude", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "Césaire/Senghor's affirmative-blackness movement; Fanon is sympathetic but critiques it as continental rather than national, hence trapped in the colonial frame"}},
    {"id": "colonized_intellectual", "label": "the Colonized Intellectual", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the figure who must journey deep into the people to find legitimate ground"}},
    {"id": "new_man", "label": "the New Man", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the post-colonial human — to be built, not waited on; the conclusion's call"}},
    {"id": "universal_humanism", "label": "Universal Humanism (false)", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the European invocation Fanon rejects; built on Manichaean exclusion in practice"}},
    {"id": "mental_disorders_as_symptom", "label": "Mental Disorders as Colonial Symptom", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "chapter V's thesis: psychiatric pathology in colonial subjects is not individual but a social-political wound"}},
    {"id": "pacification", "label": "Pacification (as euphemism)", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the colonial vocabulary's name for ongoing violence; one of 'discipline, tame, subdue, pacify'"}},
    {"id": "dehumanization", "label": "Dehumanization", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the Manichaean reduction of the colonized to animal — 'zoological terms' in colonial discourse"}},
    {"id": "eschatological_reversal", "label": "'The Last Shall Be the First'", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "Fanon's borrowed apocalyptic register; Bhabha names the 'religiosity' of his revolutionary wrath"}},
    {"id": "third_world", "label": "the Third World", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "the collective political subject Fanon addresses — meant to start a 'new history of man'"}},
    {"id": "marxist_analysis_stretched", "label": "Marxist Analysis (stretched for colonial context)", "type": "Concept", "color": "#F8F0C8", "properties": {"role": "Fanon's amendment: in the colonies, race functions as class — economic infrastructure IS racial superstructure"}},

    {"id": "fln", "label": "Front de Libération Nationale (FLN)", "type": "Movement", "color": "#E0E0E0", "properties": {"role": "Algerian liberation organization; political wing"}},
    {"id": "aln", "label": "Armée de Libération Nationale (ALN)", "type": "Movement", "color": "#E0E0E0", "properties": {"role": "FLN's military wing; treats fighters wounded in combat (case 2's S—— is rescued by ALN soldiers)"}},
    {"id": "french_army", "label": "the French Army (in Algeria)", "type": "Movement", "color": "#E0E0E0", "properties": {"role": "occupation force; agent of pacification, torture, and massacre"}},
    {"id": "colonialism", "label": "Colonialism (the system)", "type": "Movement", "color": "#E0E0E0", "properties": {"role": "the system itself, distinguished from any particular colonial state — the structure being theorized"}},
    {"id": "colonial_christianity", "label": "Colonial Christianity", "type": "Movement", "color": "#E0E0E0", "properties": {"role": "the Church in the colonies — 'a foreigners' Church'; missionizing as alienation-vector"}},
    {"id": "arab_league", "label": "the Arab League", "type": "Movement", "color": "#E0E0E0", "properties": {"role": "the supra-national Arab cultural-political body; Fanon contrasts its national heterogeneity with negritude's continental unity"}},
    {"id": "negritude_movement", "label": "the Négritude Movement", "type": "Movement", "color": "#E0E0E0", "properties": {"role": "the literary-political movement (Césaire, Senghor, Birago Diop, Hampaté Ba) producing 'black voices'"}},
    {"id": "algiers_school", "label": "the Algiers School of Psychiatry", "type": "Movement", "color": "#E0E0E0", "properties": {"role": "Porot, Sutter, et al.; produced the colonial neuropsychiatric doctrine Fanon dismantles"}},

    {"id": "algeria", "label": "Algeria", "type": "Place", "color": "#C8F8DC", "properties": {"role": "the war's site; Fanon's adopted home"}},
    {"id": "algiers", "label": "Algiers", "type": "Place", "color": "#C8F8DC", "properties": {"role": "the city; site of Porot's school and many of the case studies"}},
    {"id": "constantine_region", "label": "the Constantine Region", "type": "Place", "color": "#C8F8DC", "properties": {"role": "rural Algeria; site of Case 2's massacred douar"}},
    {"id": "france", "label": "France (metropolitan)", "type": "Place", "color": "#C8F8DC", "properties": {"role": "the metropole; the colonist's referenced home"}},
    {"id": "martinique", "label": "Martinique", "type": "Place", "color": "#C8F8DC", "properties": {"role": "Fanon's birthplace; Césaire's home; first site of his analysis of colonial racism"}},
    {"id": "kenya", "label": "Kenya", "type": "Place", "color": "#C8F8DC", "properties": {"role": "site of the Mau-Mau revolt Carothers tried to pathologize"}},

    {"id": "patient_b", "label": "B—— (case I.1)", "type": "Patient", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "26-year-old former taxi driver, FLN militant since age 18; presents with impotence and migraines after wife's rape"}},
    {"id": "patient_b_wife", "label": "B——'s wife", "type": "Person", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "raped by French soldiers during interrogation about her husband's whereabouts; 'a tenacious woman who was prepared to accept anything rather than give up her husband'"}},
    {"id": "patient_b_daughter", "label": "B——'s daughter", "type": "Person", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "twenty-month-old at time of incident; B—— develops impulse to tear up her photo"}},
    {"id": "patient_s", "label": "S—— (case I.2)", "type": "Patient", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "37-year-old fellah, Constantine douar; presents with random homicidal impulses after surviving point-blank-range mass execution"}},
    {"id": "french_officer_helicopter", "label": "the French Officer (case I.2)", "type": "Person", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "arrived by helicopter; declared 'There's too much fuss over this douar; destroy it!'; ordered the massacre"}},
    {"id": "patient_d", "label": "D—— (case I.3)", "type": "Patient", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "19-year-old former student, ALN fighter; presents with depression, hallucinations, and attempted suicide after killing a settler's wife"}},
    {"id": "patient_d_mother", "label": "D——'s mother", "type": "Person", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "killed at point-blank range by a French soldier; D——'s primary attachment"}},
    {"id": "settlers_wife", "label": "the Settler's Wife (case I.3)", "type": "Person", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "killed by D—— in her home; 'I've got children'; appears in his hallucinations as bloodless, multiplied, demanding her blood back"}},
    {"id": "patient_a", "label": "A—— (case I.4)", "type": "Patient", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "28-year-old European police officer; transferred to anti-FLN brigade; presents with auditory hallucinations of his torture victims' screams"}},
    {"id": "as_torture_victims", "label": "A——'s torture victims (collective)", "type": "Person", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "interrogated, beaten, suspended, near-drowned, electrocuted, in some cases killed at police headquarters"}},
    {"id": "patient_d_commander", "label": "D——'s commander", "type": "Person", "color": "#F8D8C8", "properties": {"role": "disarmed D—— after the killing; ordered him to leave; later interrogated him about it"}},

    {"id": "case_b_event", "label": "the rape of B——'s wife (case I.1 event)", "type": "ReifiedEvent", "color": "#DCC8F8", "properties": {"role": "n-ary reified event; the originating violence; 'It was the rape of a tenacious woman who was prepared to accept anything rather than give up her husband. And that husband was me.'"}},
    {"id": "case_s_event", "label": "the destruction of S——'s douar (case I.2 event)", "type": "ReifiedEvent", "color": "#DCC8F8", "properties": {"role": "n-ary reified event; 29 men killed at point-blank range, houses burned, women driven back with rifle butts; S—— wounded in thigh and arm"}},
    {"id": "case_d_event", "label": "D——'s killing of the settler's wife (case I.3 event)", "type": "ReifiedEvent", "color": "#DCC8F8", "properties": {"role": "n-ary reified event; ALN raid on a known-violent settler's estate; the wife alone in the house; D——'s mind on his murdered mother; the kill, the disarming, the interrogation"}},
    {"id": "case_a_event", "label": "A——'s torture sessions (case I.4 event)", "type": "ReifiedEvent", "color": "#DCC8F8", "properties": {"role": "n-ary reified event; the police-headquarters interrogation regime — punches, suspension, bathtub, electricity; A—— graduates from amusement to permanent auditory haunting"}},

    {"id": "algerian_war", "label": "the Algerian War of Independence (1954-1962)", "type": "ReifiedEvent", "color": "#DCC8F8", "properties": {"role": "the encompassing war; the whole book is its theory; n-ary participants include FLN, ALN, French Army, Algerian people, France, settlers"}},
    {"id": "bandung_conference", "label": "the Bandung Conference (1955)", "type": "ReifiedEvent", "color": "#DCC8F8", "properties": {"role": "founding moment of nonalignment; Bhabha names it as the dream now in question"}},
    {"id": "setif_massacre", "label": "the Sétif Massacre (1945)", "type": "ReifiedEvent", "color": "#DCC8F8", "properties": {"role": "French repression of Algerian VE-day demonstration; one of colonial violence's pre-war reference points"}},
    {"id": "fanon_death", "label": "Fanon's death (Dec 6, 1961)", "type": "ReifiedEvent", "color": "#DCC8F8", "properties": {"role": "leukemia; treated at NIH Bethesda under nom-de-guerre 'Ibrahim Fanon'; French police seized the book in Paris that same day"}},
    {"id": "mau_mau_revolt", "label": "the Mau-Mau Revolt (Kenya)", "type": "ReifiedEvent", "color": "#DCC8F8", "properties": {"role": "Kenyan anti-colonial revolt; Carothers diagnosed it as a 'frustration complex'"}}
  ],
